@title How retries and timeouts compound on a ferry booking
@subtitle Why a slow vessel-status service turns into a pile-up, and where the worst-case wait comes from.
@width wide

!! Not checked against the code. You asked me to compare the production settings dump with what the code really does. I did not read the repo, so every statement below comes from the dump and the incident figures you gave me. Where the dump and the code disagree, the dump is the less trustworthy source for the question "what will the next deploy do", and the code (plus any environment overrides) is the less trustworthy source for "what was running last week". The checklist at the end tells you what to look up.

kpi Load balancer cut-off | 30 s | what the customer waits at most
kpi booking-api worst case | 30.6 s | longer than the cut-off | bad
kpi vessel-status worst case | 23.75 s | 5 tries of 4 s plus waits
kpi Vessel-status calls per seat check | up to 15 | 3 x 5 | bad

## The mechanism in five steps

Each service has a retry policy: how many attempts, how long to wait for each one (the timeout per try), and how long to pause between attempts (the delay, which grows by the multiplier each time and is randomized by the jitter). The key point is that a slow service is worse than a dead one. A dead service fails instantly and the caller gives up quickly. A slow service makes every attempt run to its full timeout, and then the retry policy launches another.

1. vessel-status gets slower than its 4 s per-try timeout. Every call now burns the full 4 s and then fails.
2. Each caller's policy turns one request into several. vessel-status is retried up to 5 times, and it has 0% jitter, so clients that failed together retry together, in waves.
3. seat-inventory calls vessel-status. seat-inventory gives up on its own 5 s try, but the vessel-status work it started keeps running. Then seat-inventory starts try 2 and try 3, each with its own batch of up to 5 calls. One seat check can leave up to 15 requests on a service that is already struggling.
4. That extra load keeps vessel-status slow, so more calls time out, so more retries are sent. This is the pile-up.
5. At 30 s the load balancer drops the customer, who clicks "Book now" again. The old work is still running, and a fresh set starts.

## Who calls whom

!! Assumption: I read each row of the dump as the policy that callers use when they call that service. So vessel-status 5 x 4 s is how seat-inventory (and possibly booking-api) calls vessel-status. Your dump says booking-api "calls the next three"; I took that to mean the three rows after it (fare-calculator, vessel-status, seat-inventory), and I assumed payment-gateway is in the booking path too. If instead each row is the policy a service uses for its own outbound calls, the chain through seat-inventory is 3 x 3 = 9 calls, not 15. The code settles both questions.

