flow: user "Customer" user "clicks Book now"
flow: lb "Load balancer" lb "cuts at 30 s"
flow: booking "booking-api" svc "3 x 10 s"
flow: fare "fare-calculator" svc "4 x 3 s"
flow: seat "seat-inventory" svc "3 x 5 s"
flow: vessel "vessel-status" svc "5 x 4 s, no jitter" focus
flow: pay "payment-gateway" ext "2 x 15 s"
flow: notif "notification-svc" queue "6 x 5 s"
flow: loyalty "loyalty-points" svc "3 x 6 s"
flow: user -> lb
flow: lb -> booking
flow: booking -> fare, seat, pay
flow: seat -> vessel : capacity
flow: booking --> vessel : direct?
flow: booking --> notif : async
flow: booking --> loyalty : unclear

The nightly port-schedule-sync and the refund-worker are background jobs and are not on the "Book now" path as far as the dump says.

## Where the worst-case wait comes from

For one service the worst case is attempts x timeout per try, plus the pauses between attempts. I ignored jitter, which adds at most 10 to 30% to the pauses and so changes little. For the services on the booking path:

data worst = Service,Waiting on timeouts (s),Pausing between tries (s)
data worst += booking-api,30,0.6
data worst += fare-calculator,12,0.475
data worst += vessel-status,20,3.75
data worst += seat-inventory,15,0.45
data worst += payment-gateway,30,0.5
data worst += loyalty-points,18,0.9
chart hbar worst stack unit=s labels title="Worst case per service: nearly all of it is waiting on timeouts"

The wait comes from the timeouts, not from the backoff delays. The delays, the part people tune, are small by comparison.

Each service on its own, including the background jobs (my arithmetic from the dump):

data calc = Service,Attempts,Timeouts (s),Pauses (s),Worst case (s),Largest pause (s),Max Delay cap (s),Cap reached
data calc += booking-api,3,30,0.6,30.6,0.4,5,No
data calc += fare-calculator,4,12,0.475,12.475,0.225,2,No
data calc += vessel-status,5,20,3.75,23.75,2,8,No
data calc += seat-inventory,3,15,0.45,15.45,0.3,3,No
data calc += payment-gateway,2,30,0.5,30.5,0.5,10,No
data calc += notification-svc,6,30,31,61,16,60,No
data calc += port-schedule-sync,8,160,64.34,224.34,22.78,120,No
data calc += loyalty-points,3,18,0.9,18.9,0.6,4,No
data calc += refund-worker,5,60,19.03,79.03,11.72,30,No
table calc heat=Worst case (s)

### How the retries multiply

Nested retries multiply instead of adding. Here is one booking-api request to seat-inventory, with vessel-status hanging on every call and the vessel-status work never cancelled once seat-inventory gives up on its try:

data spans = Span,Start,End
data spans += seat-inventory try 1,0,5
data spans += seat-inventory try 2,5.15,10.15
data spans += seat-inventory try 3,10.45,15.45
data spans += vessel-status work from try 1,0,23.75
data spans += vessel-status work from try 2,5.15,28.9
data spans += vessel-status work from try 3,10.45,34.2
chart range spans from=Start to=End target="30 LB cut-off" title="Seconds since the first call: work outlives the customer's wait"

The seat-inventory tries end by 15.45 s. The vessel-status work they started goes on until 34.2 s, past the load balancer's cut-off, and at around 10.5 s all three batches overlap on the slow service.

data amp = Layer,Attempts,Calls reaching vessel-status
data amp += One seat-inventory try,5,5
data amp += booking-api asking seat-inventory,3,15
table amp

If cancellation propagates, so that a try giving up cancels the calls under it, only about 2 of the 5 calls start inside each 5 s try, so 6 instead of 15 (mine). Whether it propagates is a code question. If booking-api also calls vessel-status directly, add up to 5 more per attempt.

## What the incident figures say

On Sept 3 the booking error rate reached 38% for 41 minutes and p99 latency was 74 s against a normal 1.9 s.

!! The 74 s does not fit a 30 s load balancer cut-off unless it was measured inside booking-api rather than at the load balancer. If so, that is the orphaned work above showing up in the metrics. Adding up the dump's worst cases for a sequential walk (fare-calculator 12.5 s, vessel-status 23.75 s, seat-inventory 15.45 s, payment-gateway 30.5 s) gives about 82 s, which is in the same range as 74 s. I have not verified where p99 was measured or whether the calls are sequential.

## Settings that look like mistakes or leftovers

data flags = Setting,Why it looks wrong,Confidence
data flags += vessel-status jitter 0%,"The note says “temporary” since the March outage. With no jitter, retries from many clients land together, which makes a pile-up worse. It was still in place on Sept 3.",High
data flags += Max Delay on every service,"None of the nine caps is ever reached. The largest pause on each service is well under its cap (for example vessel-status pauses top out at 2 s against a cap of 8 s). The caps look like leftovers from earlier attempt counts, or they do nothing.",High
data flags += booking-api 3 x 10 s,"Worst case 30.6 s, just over the load balancer's 30 s. The third attempt cannot finish usefully, and the work keeps running after the customer is cut off.",High
data flags += seat-inventory 5 s per try over vessel-status 23.75 s,"The outer timeout is shorter than the inner worst case, so inner work is orphaned and re-launched on every outer retry.",High
data flags += payment-gateway 2 x 15 s,"Worst case 30.5 s, past the load balancer cut-off. If a customer is cut off mid-payment and clicks again, check that payments are safe to repeat (idempotent), or there is a double-charge risk.",Needs the code
data flags += notification-svc 6 attempts and up to 61 s,"The note says it is async and should not block bookings. Confirm it really is a queue and not awaited.",Needs the code
data flags += loyalty-points 3 x 6 s with failures swallowed,"If it is called inline, a booking can wait up to 18.9 s on something whose failure is ignored anyway (see OPS-5521).",Needs the code
data flags += port-schedule-sync blank jitter,"Blank is not the same as 0. It may mean a default applies. Worst case is 224 s, which is fine for a nightly job.",Low
table flags

